# Development — Retrospective

When an epic or feature closes, capture what was actually learned while it is
fresh, and convert the durable lessons into steering so the next feature does not
repeat the same mistakes. A retro that changes nothing is theater.

## Goal

Produce `specs/<feature>/retrospective.md` and, from it, concrete updates to
steering (and the workflow itself) so the team's defaults improve.

## Default Operation Mode

Direct, evidence-based. You ground the retro in what actually happened —
`sprint-status.yaml`, the git history, review findings — not vibes.

## Collect Inputs First

- Which epic/feature is closing? Confirm its tasks are `done` in
  `sprint-status.yaml`.
- What is the evidence? Blockers logged, tasks that slipped, review themes,
  surprises during `tdd-cycle`.

## Execution Workflow

### 1) Gather the facts
Pull the timeline from `sprint-status.yaml` and git log: what slipped, where
blockers clustered, which reviews kept finding the same class of issue.

### 2) Run the retro
Fill `assets/retrospective-template.md`: what worked (keep), what hurt (stop),
what to try (start). Tie each item to evidence, not impressions.

### 3) Convert lessons to action
For each durable lesson, decide its home:
- A recurring technical mistake → update `.claude/steering/tech.md` or
  `structure.md` (run `csdd steering validate` after).
- A process gap → note it against the relevant `wf:development` phase.
- A one-off → record it and move on; do not over-generalize.

### 4) Write and close
Write `specs/<feature>/retrospective.md` with the actions and their owners.

## Gotchas

- A lesson with no resulting change is not a lesson. Every "what hurt" needs a
  "what we will do differently".
- Update steering only for patterns, not one-offs — steering is durable memory,
  and noise dilutes it.
- Keep it blameless and specific. "Tests were flaky" → which tests, why, what
  changes.
- Do not let the retro reopen the work. Capture follow-ups as new spec/tasks, not
  edits to the closed feature.

## Verification Before Reporting

- Run: `csdd steering validate` if you touched steering.
- Check: every "what hurt" has a concrete action with an owner; steering edits
  capture patterns, not noise.
- If blocked: if the evidence is too thin to draw lessons, say so rather than
  inventing insights.

## Completion Criteria
- [ ] `specs/<feature>/retrospective.md` exists with keep / stop / start items.
- [ ] Each pain point maps to a concrete action and owner.
- [ ] Durable technical lessons are folded into steering (validated).
- [ ] Follow-up work is captured as new specs/tasks, not edits to closed work.

## References

- Use `assets/retrospective-template.md` for the retro structure.
